>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is corporate law a good career? It is a good one for the person who can reach a deal-heavy or advisory seat, likes precise, deadline-driven work and plans the next move early. It is a weak default for anyone choosing it only because it sounds like the high-pay side of law.</strong>
    The label hides several different lives: a top-firm associate, an in-house lawyer, a compliance adviser and an owner of a small boutique. The decisions you make about route, hiring gates and add-on skills decide whether you build a <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> and move toward earlier financial freedom, or a few years of long hours with no plan.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The money is strongest at the top firms, but the seats are few and the hours are long. Salaried firm and in-house routes are rated Not so scalable because both follow a fixed ladder with a narrow top.</li>
      <li>The routes that scale are compliance advisory, then a boutique or independent practice built after you have clients and a niche. We lead with those, then list the crowded routes with their risk named.</li>
      <li>Many top-firm jobs are decided by internships and pre-placement offers before the final year. Without that pipeline, mid-size firms, in-house roles and compliance work are realistic first targets.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> pick one practice area, draft one sample document and show it to three people in the route, before you spend on an LLM or coaching.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The verdict in one screen: is corporate law a good career</h2>

  <p>
    Most pages answer this with a salary and a yes. A better answer depends on three things: which route you can actually enter, what a normal week costs you, and what the route can grow into.
  </p>

  <p>
    A law degree is a legal requirement for court practice, so this is a field where the degree matters. The problem is stopping at the degree with no skill built on top of it. This page covers only the corporate side of law. For the wider map of options by degree stage, read <a href="/blog/career-options/law-career-options/">law career options after LLB</a>. For the whole field, including courts and the judiciary, read <a href="/blog/career-options/is-law-a-good-career-in-india/">is law a good career in India</a>. The wider <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a> guides sit beside them.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Corporate law has two filters, not one. The first is getting a seat in a strong team. The second is the climb after it, where one large firm reported 184 associate promotions and 17 new partners in a single year. Most people who clear the first filter do not reach the top of the second, so plan an exit or a business layer from the start, not at year eight.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="work-areas">Corporate law is eight kinds of work</h2>

  <p>
    Pick the work before the title. The order below puts the areas that can sell a skill directly first, then the deal-team areas that scale mostly through a later move.
  </p>

  <div class="legend">
    <span class="tag tag--teal">Scalable</span>
    <span class="tag tag--gold">Scalable with a business layer</span>
    <span class="tag tag--red">Not so scalable</span>
  </div>

