  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The same table shows the gap. A top National Law University median sits near Rs 18 to 20 lakh, while some newer ones sit below Rs 10 lakh, with low placement shares. The corporate firm number families quote belongs to the top slice of each batch. Check the placement report of your own college, including how many students were in the denominator, before you assume it applies to you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="hours">Hours, attrition and burnout</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part career pages skip. Hours depend on the team, the partner and the deal calendar, so no single average is honest.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the evidence shows</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Vahura's 2022 survey of over 1,450 professionals at over 300 firms found over 50 percent looking to move on, with long hours a main reason (<a href="https://www.barandbench.com/news/prioritizing-mental-health-enabling-safe-spaces-regulating-work-hours-how-law-firms-in-india-can-curb-attrition-and-attract-talent" rel="noopener">Bar and Bench</a>).</li>
        <li>About 45 percent of those working 10 to 12 hours a day said they felt routinely stressed. Only 18 percent of that group said their firms encouraged work-life balance.</li>
        <li>Mint reported in June 2026 that firms were battling high churn, which is why many are paying higher increments, with Vahura expecting 10 to 20 percent this year.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">How to protect yourself</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Before you accept an offer, ask two current juniors in that team about a normal month and a deal month.</li>
        <li>Choose the team, not only the firm. Deal teams, disputes teams and regulatory teams run on different rhythms.</li>
        <li>Plan the exit rung early: in-house, compliance advisory or a boutique. Do not wait until you are exhausted.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The survey is from 2022 and is the closest firm-level data we found. Treat it as a signal, not a current average.
  </p>

  <h2 id="partner-track">The partner track and lateral moves</h2>

  <p>
    A top-firm career is a ladder with rungs measured in post-qualification experience (PQE), meaning years since you qualified. These bands are one 2026 guide's planning anchors, and firms differ.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Rung</th>
          <th>Typical PQE</th>
          <th>What changes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ladderRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Rung"><strong>{item.rung}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Typical PQE">{item.pqe}</td>
            <td data-label="What changes">{item.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: <a href="https://blog.ipleaders.in/law-firm-partnership-track-in-india-associate-to-partner-roadmap-2026/" rel="noopener">iPleaders partnership roadmap, verified 30 April 2026</a>. The guide itself says its bands overlap and are not fixed gates.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Equity is a second job.</strong> An equity partner owns part of the business, shares in profit and loss and is expected to bring clients. That is why some strong senior lawyers choose in-house or a boutique instead of waiting for the last rung. A Spencer Stuart study of Indian general counsel also noted that equity can be hard to obtain in family-run firms.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    On lateral moves, the same guide advises against switching firms too early, because it resets your technical credibility, and calls the middle years, around PQE 5 to 7, the common window. Treat that as one guide's advice. The move that matters more is the one you plan: to a firm with a stronger practice, to an in-house team, or to your own work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-house">The in-house ladder</h2>

  <p>
    In-house means being the lawyer for one company. A study of 150 Indian companies found more than 90 percent had a general counsel or head of legal, and almost 36 percent of general counsel began at a law firm (<a href="https://www.spencerstuart.com/research-and-insight/general-counsel-in-india-the-route-to-the-top" rel="noopener">Spencer Stuart, 2021</a>). Titles differ by company, but the ladder usually looks like this.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {inHouseSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The same study found fewer than 40 percent of companies had a general counsel with a seat at the executive team or board, so the top post is not always a strategy seat. Moving in-house also means giving up the right to practise in court, as a full-time salaried lawyer, and that is hard to undo. Many practitioners say in-house hours are steadier, but month-end and deal peaks still happen, so treat that as a tendency, not a promise.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pay">Pay: what the sources support</h2>

  <p>
    We use only figures that a named source supports, and we leave out the rest. The college medians above are the most solid fresher numbers we found. For later years, partner and general counsel pay figures online come from recruiters, forum threads and unsourced estimates, so we do not print them here.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Fresher outcomes track the college. Compare the medians and placement shares in the table above, not a firm's headline offer.</li>
    <li>Pay has been rising. Vahura told Mint it expects increments of 10 to 20 percent in 2026, against 7 to 10 percent the year before.</li>
    <li>Increments are tied to performance. Top performers may see 15 to 20 percent, sometimes with a leaner fixed pay and a larger bonus.</li>
    <li>Corporate India spent an estimated Rs 69,000 to 72,000 crore on legal matters in FY26, up from about Rs 60,000 crore the year before, according to Mint's earlier reporting. That is the demand behind the hiring.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public profiles and press reports, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year, and markets change. Check current job listings (for example on LinkedIn, Naukri or Indeed) and ask people working in the role before you decide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-juniors">AI and the junior work</h2>

  <p>
    The first work to shrink is the work freshers used to do: document review, diligence data entry and first-draft research. In December 2025 Mint reported that Cyril Amarchand Mangaldas said over 80 percent of its headcount used AI and that it aimed for 90 percent or more. Firms told Mint that time on research and documentation fell by 20 to 30 percent, and by 40 to 60 percent in some document-heavy work (<a href="https://www.livemint.com/ai/artificial-intelligence/indian-legal-industry-ai-transformation-billing-models-judiciary-oversight-11765885915833.html" rel="noopener">Mint, 29 December 2025</a>).
  </p>

  <p>
    Opinions on hiring differ. Khaitan said it is building teams that mix law and technology, and a founding partner at CMS IndusLaw expects hybrid roles, not smaller teams. A partner at Pioneer Legal, a 40-lawyer firm, told Mint in June 2026 that firms are looking to reduce fresher hiring as AI use grows. The honest read: the number of entry seats may be flat or shrinking, and the bar for the seats that remain is higher.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
